{
  "slug": "posesivos",
  "number": 8,
  "part": 2,
  "title": "Mon, ma, mes",
  "tagline": "Possessives agree with the thing owned, not the owner: the big «his or her» trap.",
  "goal": "Say whose things are, talk about your family and stop choosing son or sa according to the owner, which is what English «his» and «her» train you to do.",
  "minutes": 8,
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "tabla",
      "title": "The possessives",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Owner",
            "Masculine singular",
            "Feminine singular",
            "Plural"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "my (je)",
              "*{mon} livre*",
              "*{ma} maison*",
              "*{mes} amis*"
            ],
            [
              "your (tu)",
              "*{ton} livre*",
              "*{ta} maison*",
              "*{tes} amis*"
            ],
            [
              "his, her, its (il, elle)",
              "*{son} livre*",
              "*{sa} maison*",
              "*{ses} amis*"
            ],
            [
              "our (nous)",
              "*{notre} livre*",
              "*{notre} maison*",
              "*{nos} amis*"
            ],
            [
              "your (vous)",
              "*{votre} livre*",
              "*{votre} maison*",
              "*{vos} amis*"
            ],
            [
              "their (ils, elles)",
              "*{leur} livre*",
              "*{leur} maison*",
              "*{leurs} amis*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "text": "*notre, votre* and *leur* don't change with gender, only with plural."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "objeto",
      "title": "It agrees with the thing owned",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In English, «his» or «her» depends on **the owner**. In French, the possessive agrees with **the thing owned** [[2]]:"
        },
        {
          "type": "examples",
          "items": [
            {
              "fr": "Paul aime sa mère.",
              "tr": "Paul loves his mother.",
              "note": "sa because mère is feminine, even though Paul is a man"
            },
            {
              "fr": "Marie aime son père.",
              "tr": "Marie loves her father.",
              "note": "son because père is masculine"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "warning",
          "text": "*sa* does not mean «her» and *son* does not mean «his». Both mean «his» **or** «her»; the context tells you who the owner is. Before choosing, ask yourself: is the thing masculine, feminine or plural?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "mon-amie",
      "title": "Mon amie",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Before a **feminine word starting with a vowel or silent h**, use **mon, ton, son** instead of *ma, ta, sa*, to avoid two vowels clashing:"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Wrong",
            "Right"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "*ma amie*",
              "*{mon} amie*"
            ],
            [
              "*ta école*",
              "*{ton} école*"
            ],
            [
              "*sa histoire*",
              "*{son} histoire*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Same logic as *cet* [[3]]."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "su",
      "title": "Your and their",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "English «your» covers one person or several, formal or not. French separates them:"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "English",
            "Meaning",
            "French"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "your book",
              "a friend, family (tu)",
              "*ton livre*"
            ],
            [
              "your book",
              "someone you address formally (vous)",
              "*votre livre*"
            ],
            [
              "your book",
              "several people (vous)",
              "*votre livre*"
            ],
            [
              "their book",
              "belonging to them",
              "*leur livre*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "warning",
          "text": "Match the possessive to how you address the person: if you say *vous* to someone, it's *votre*. «Madam, is this your umbrella?» → *Madame, c'est votre parapluie ?*"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "tip",
          "title": "leur or leurs",
          "text": "*leur* = one thing belonging to several people (*leur maison*, their house). *leurs* = several things (*leurs enfants*, their children)."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
